A||||5||btoth||Handling questions||||5||
Z||000000||btoth||05-23-2001||04:59 PM||||OK, I got my 86 GT back Monday from getting a new engine installed (another 2.8).<P>Since I've had it, it has been drifting to the right and the back end feels really sloppy in the turns... almost like it moves too much.  (Bad description.)<P>The shop said that they did a rear alignment (and I trust that they did).  And they didn't have any reason to mess with the front.  But I don't recall it having these problems before it died (which was almost two months ago).<P>They did get new bolts for the back struts.  They gave the old bolts back.  Could they maybe have not tightened them down properly? Or is it possible that maybe they just don't align things like the tire place does?  Could the tire place have had it set for more camber and that's what I'm noticing?<P>Is there a better way that I can tell if it's in need of a 4-wheel alignment or just new suspension?<P>Sorry for all the ?'s... any thoughts would be appreciated.  My parents are sick of hearing about the car I think.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||05-23-2001||05:25 PM||||Chances are they put a gauge on the wheel that tells you where to put the alignment when you put the car back together. They probably used this gauge instead of the alignment rack, which cost money.<P>Take it back and complain. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||danny boy||05-23-2001||06:00 PM||||Its amazing how much an alignment in the rear can throw off the balance of the early cars. The feeling you are experiencing- like the car is steering with the rear wheels? Very loose and twitchy? that would be my guess,too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||btoth||05-24-2001||12:39 PM||||Well, I was having a 4-wheel alignment done today, and the guy just called and said that it's not my alignent... he said my rear inner and outer tie rods are completely shot.  OK.  But he said that they won't replace them on a Fiero!  WTF!?  And of course he mentioned that the parts would be "hard to find" because of it being a Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> Well, the Fiero Store sells them of course.<P>Could they have got damaged durring the installation of the new engine??  This is pi$$ing me off because my car used to handle really well and lately it's just one thing after another.<P>And where would you recomend getting new ones form?  The Fiero Store?  Or somewhere else?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||05-24-2001||03:48 PM||||Get the car home and see if there is any play in the rear wheels. Jack the back of the car up and try to wiggle the tire. If they don't move, then the alignment guy is likely trying to scam you, if they do, then check around for a good price on them. the rear outer tie rod ends are easy to come by and cost about 45 bucks depending on where you get them. The inner ones are the hard ones to get but you might luck out and find a set at autozone or NAPA.<P>The problem you're having can be caused by bad motor mounts and a arm bushings too so you might want to check those while you're at it. It would also explain why it happened after the engine swap.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||05-25-2001||12:14 AM||||Hey Btoth!<BR>If they did not throw it on the rack and perform a complete 4 wheel alignment, go back and complain...<BR>Your car probly dog tracks now...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
